  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Pokot\"\nsubtitle: \"Kenya\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Pokot, also called Pökoot, are a Southern Nilotic people among the Kalenjin-speaking peoples. |\n| Where | West Pokot and Baringo Counties in Kenya, and the Pokot District of eastern Karamoja in Uganda. |\n| How many | 778,408 in Kenya in 2019, plus more than 140,000 estimated in Uganda. |\n| Language | Pökoot, a northern Kalenjin language. |\n |\n| Known for | Pastoral and agricultural life · Age-based social groups · Cattle raiding · Proverbs, riddles, and tales · Distinctive clothing and beadwork |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Pokot live mainly in western Kenya and eastern Uganda. They speak Pökoot, a northern Kalenjin language. Their cultural record includes pastoral and agricultural life, age groups, cattle raiding, oral art, and distinctive clothing and adornment. Museum collections show a wide range of leather, bead, wood, gourd, basket, and metal objects.\n\n## Material culture\n\nPokot material culture uses leather, skin, beads, cowrie shells, hair, feathers, gourds, wood, reeds, fibre, and metal.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nThe sources do not name Pokot textile traditions, but they record many bead-decorated garments and accessories.\n\n- **Beaded loin cloth** — A leather loin cloth has rows of beads and cowrie shells, with beaded straps tied around the waist.\n- **Beaded women’s belt** — A skin belt has a broad central band, tie thongs, cowrie shells, cotton thread, and rows of colored plastic beads.\n- **Beaded women’s bag** — A rectangular cow-skin bag has hair attached, a fold-over top, and a strap decorated with cowrie shells and plastic beads.\n- **Beaded head ornament** — Four strings of red, green, yellow, and black beads are twisted together and tied around the forehead.\n- **Beaded collar** — Collars use several strings of colored plastic beads arranged in bands on metal wire or twisted vegetable fibre.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nPokot clothing and dress accessories use leather, skin, beads, hair, feathers, and red ochre.\n\n- **Man’s pubic cover** — A leather pubic cover is decorated with iron beads and coated with red ochre.\n- **Red leather cap** — A red cap is made from folded strips of oxhide.\n- **Feathered hair head-dress** — A felted human-hair head-dress is decorated with four large feathers.\n- **War head-dress** — A tightly fitting head-dress of woven human hair has a crest of black and white ostrich plumes.\n- **Hair helmet** — A wig-like helmet of matted human hair has side-flaps, a gourd pendant, and ostrich feathers in cane settings.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nPokot everyday objects include pottery, gourds, wooden vessels, baskets, knives, bells, and a war-horn.\n\n- **Handled pottery vessel** — A brownish pottery vessel has a rounded bottom, two handles, and black scorch marks.\n- **Gourd milk container** — A long slender gourd container has a lid, skin strap, beads, and palm-stalk and palm-fibre repairs.\n- **Wooden water bowl** — An elliptical wooden bowl has a rounded bottom, a wire repair, a blackened interior base, and a plaited leather loop.\n- **Grain basket** — A composite reed basket with a leather handle is used for grain.\n- **Iron finger-knife** — An iron finger-knife has a kidney-shaped or other blade, sometimes bound with copper wire.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nPokot adornment combines beads, shells, glass, bone, hardwood, feathers, hair, wire, and fibre.\n\n- **Man’s ear ornament** — A man may wear several ear ornaments in the same ear.\n- **Banana-seed necklace** — One necklace is made from banana seeds.\n- **Bone armlet** — One recorded armlet is made from bone.\n- **Young women’s collar** — A banded bead collar is described as worn by young married women or unmarried girls.\n- **Beaded neck ornament** — A neck ornament uses strips of hardwood and white and blue glass beads strung on fibre thread.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nThe sources give little information about music, but they record a side-blown war-horn and a strong verbal performance tradition.\n\n- **War-horn** — A side-blown horn is made of wood covered with strips of hide.\n- **Elder proverb speaking** — At gatherings of elders, speakers use proverbs to show skill in speaking and make a point.\n- **Lökoi** — Lökoi is speech about news from other places.\n- **Chiran** — Chiran is speech about events in the neighborhood.\n- **Kokwö** — Kokwö is serious, business-like conversation.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe sources describe age groups, circumcision, and initiation, but no annual festival calendar.\n\n- **Karachna** — Karachna is the social grouping for boys.\n- **Muren** — Muren is the social grouping for circumcised men.\n- **Poi** — Poi is the social grouping for old men.\n- **Age-set joining** — Boys joined an age-set after circumcision.\n- **Girls’ circumcision controversy** — In 2014, pictures of the circumcision of young Pokot girls caused public outrage abroad, and Kenya had legally banned the practice.\n\n## Foodways\n\nThe sources do not name Pokot dishes or cooking traditions, but they record containers for milk, honey, sheep fat, and grain.\n\n- **Milk gourd** — A long gourd container is used to store milk.\n- **Honey and sheep-fat container** — A hollowed wooden container with a leather bottom and lid is used for sheep fat or honey, especially honey.\n- **Grain basket** — A composite basket is used for grain.\n- **Cattle bell** — A cattle-bell is used on a bull.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nPokot verbal art uses proverbs, riddles, and tales to teach, argue, entertain, and show speaking skill.\n\n- **Moral proverbs** — Proverbs teach younger people the consequences of leaving the moral path.\n- **Louwialan clan tale** (*Louwialan*) — A popular tale about the Louwialan clan warns against pride.\n- **Blind girl tale** — Another common tale concerns a blind girl who returns from death.\n- **Riddles** (*Tyangoi*) — Tyangoi riddles sharpen children’s wits and hold their attention during storytelling.\n- **Old-times talk** (*Ng'öliontoköny*) — Ng'öliontoköny is talk about olden times.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nPökoot is a northern Kalenjin language spoken in western Kenya and eastern Uganda, while the supplied sources give no detailed account of religion.\n\n- **Pökoot language** (*Pökoot*) — Pökoot, also called Pokot, Päkot, Pökot, or Suk in older literature, is spoken in western Kenya and eastern Uganda.\n- **Kimukon** — Other Kalenjin peoples usually call the Pökoot Kimukon.\n- **Kalenjin language family** — Pökoot belongs to the northern branch of the Kalenjin languages found in Kenya, Uganda, and Tanzania.\n- **Neighboring language area** — The language area borders Karimojong, Turkana, Maa languages, Tugen, and Markweta.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Pökoot* — The Pokot language\n- *Päkot* — A name for the Pokot language\n- *Pökot* — A name for the Pokot language\n- *Suk* — Older name for the Pokot language\n- *Kimukon* — Name used by other Kalenjin peoples\n- *Lökoi* — News of other places\n- *Chiran* — News from the neighborhood\n- *Kokwö* — Serious business-like talk\n- *Ng'öliontoköny* — Talk of olden times\n- *Tyangoi* — Riddles\n- *Karachna* — Boys’ social grouping\n- *Muren* — Circumcised men’s grouping\n- *Poi* — Old men’s grouping\n- *Louwialan* — A clan in a popular tale\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- M. W. H. Beech, *The Suk*, Oxford: Clarendon Press, 1911.\n- Matthias Schladt, *Kognitive Strukturen von Körperteilvokabularien in Kenianischen Sprachen*, Afrikanistische Monographien, volume 8, Köln: Institut für Afrikanistik / Universität zu Köln, 1997.\n- Harold K. Schneider, *The Pakot (Suk) of Kenya, with Special Reference to the Role of Livestock in Their Subsistence Economy*, PhD Dissertation, Northwestern University, 1953.\n- Kjartan Jonsson, *Pokot Masculinity, The Role of Rituals in Forming Men*, PhD dissertation, Reykjavik: University of Iceland, Faculty of Social Sciences, 2006.\n- [Pokot people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pokot_people)\n- [Pokot language](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pokot_language)\n- British Museum and Museum of World Culture catalogue records for Pokot and Suk shields, knives, clothing, adornment, containers, tools, and musical objects.\n",
